# Item 15 phase 2. Phase-1 survey (TODOS.md, 2026-07-19): core.autocrlf=true set
# at SYSTEM level on the author's machine, no .gitattributes in the repo, and of
# the 50 tracked files under results/ 34 are all-CRLF text and 16 binary, with
# zero mixed-ending files on disk.
#
# Without this file, what lands in a commit depends on each contributor's
# personal core.autocrlf. That is the reproducibility hazard behind G20
# (committed artifacts have come from interpreters on different operating
# systems). Declaring it here makes normalisation a property of the REPO rather
# than of whoever happens to be committing.

# Default: git decides text vs binary, and stores text as LF in the index.
# Working-copy endings still follow the platform on checkout.
* text=auto

# Evidence artifacts are byte-compared across machines and quoted line-by-line
# in docs/VERIFIED-FACTS.md, so pin them to LF in the working copy too. A
# result.json must hash the same on Windows and Linux.
results/**/*.json  text eol=lf
results/**/*.md    text eol=lf
results/**/*.csv   text eol=lf
results/**/*.yaml  text eol=lf

# Source and docs: LF everywhere, so diffs never turn into whole-file rewrites.
*.py    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.tex   text eol=lf
*.bib   text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.cfg   text eol=lf

# Never touch these.
*.png   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.pyc   binary
*.npy   binary
*.npz   binary

# NOTE: existing tracked files are deliberately NOT renormalised here. Running
# `git add --renormalize .` would rewrite ~34 committed result artifacts in one
# commit purely to change line endings. Those files are evidence; churning their
# bytes for cosmetics buries the runs that produced them in the history. The
# rules above govern everything committed from now on. If a full renormalisation
# is ever wanted, do it as its own commit that touches nothing else.
